  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">New Multicultural Identities in Europe shows early scholarly uptake and clear reader interest: OpenAlex records 1 citation, while OAPEN reports 2,334 downloads. Its themes also align with SDG 10, Reduced inequalities, suggesting relevance beyond a purely academic audience.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2014, Erkan Toğuşlu’s book examines multicultural identities in Europe through questions of religion, transnational change, and post-migrant society. The record points to a work positioned at the intersection of sociology, political science, transnationalism, and multiculturalism, with an emphasis on how social and economic transitions reshape ethnic and religious identification.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest evidence of impact is usage. OAPEN download metrics show sustained reader engagement, with 2,334 downloads recorded for the open book version. That level of access indicates that the book has reached a substantial audience for a scholarly monograph, even though the citation record is still modest.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scholarly citation evidence remains limited but real: OpenAlex records one citing work, published in 2020 in The British Accounting Review. Because the citation base is very small, this should be read as an early signal of uptake rather than a broad measure of field influence. The citing landscape suggests that the book’s discussion of religion and identity has resonated beyond its core subject area.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The broader record also supports policy and societal relevance. Its alignment with Reduced inequalities fits the book’s attention to multiculturalism, inclusion, and the regulation of difference in European societies. Open access availability likely helps this reach, making the book easier to discover and reuse in research, teaching, and public-interest contexts.</p>",
